<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the notarized macOS build.

  Notarization requires the hardened runtime (codesign's "options runtime"
  flag), and the hardened runtime denies JIT by default. isomer needs JIT: it
  compiles its rules to WebAssembly and runs them through wasmtime/cranelift,
  which maps executable pages at load time. Without allow-jit, a
  hardened-runtime build dies the first time a ruleset loads, i.e. on anything
  that actually scans.

  allow-jit alone is not enough. A binary signed and notarized with only
  allow-jit (spctl reports "Notarized Developer ID") runs the version and help
  flags fine, yet any actual scan is killed in about a second with SIGKILL
  ("Code Signature Invalid", CODESIGNING / Invalid Page). cranelift maps
  executable pages that MAP_JIT plus pthread_jit_write_protect_np does not
  cover, and allow-jit cannot authorize them on its own. Both keys are required.

  This is also why a release smoke of the version and help flags proves nothing
  about signing: neither compiles a ruleset, so neither touches the JIT, and
  both pass even when the entitlements are wrong. Only a real scan exercises it.

  Keep this comment free of doubled hyphens. They are illegal inside an XML
  comment, and codesign's AMFI parser rejects the whole file ("Failed to parse
  entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error") even though plutil accepts it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
